// ── Core Bitwise Operations & Mask Idioms ─────────────────────
const a = 0b1010; // 10
const b = 0b1100; // 12

console.log((a & b).toString(2));    // '1000' = 8   (AND)
console.log((a | b).toString(2));    // '1110' = 14  (OR)
console.log((a ^ b).toString(2));    // '0110' = 6   (XOR)
console.log((~a >>> 0).toString(2)); // all-ones except positions 1,3 (NOT + unsigned)

// ── The four fundamental bit operations using (1 << k) masks ──

// 1. TEST: is bit k set?
const isBitSet = (n, k) => (n & (1 << k)) !== 0;
console.log(isBitSet(0b1010, 3)); // true  — bit 3 = value 8 is set
console.log(isBitSet(0b1010, 2)); // false — bit 2 = value 4 is NOT set

// 2. SET: force bit k ON
const setBit = (n, k) => n | (1 << k);
console.log(setBit(0b1010, 2).toString(2)); // '1110'

// 3. CLEAR: force bit k OFF
const clearBit = (n, k) => n & ~(1 << k);
console.log(clearBit(0b1010, 3).toString(2)); // '0010'

// 4. TOGGLE: flip bit k
const toggleBit = (n, k) => n ^ (1 << k);
console.log(toggleBit(0b1010, 0).toString(2)); // '1011'

// ── Flag-based permissions (Unix-style) ───────────────────────
const READ    = 1 << 2; // 0b100 = 4
const WRITE   = 1 << 1; // 0b010 = 2
const EXECUTE = 1 << 0; // 0b001 = 1

let perms = READ | WRITE; // 0b110 = 6
console.log(!!(perms & READ));    // true  — has read
console.log(!!(perms & EXECUTE)); // false — no execute
perms = clearBit(perms, 1);       // revoke WRITE
console.log(perms.toString(2));   // '100' — only READ remains

sections.content:
Counting the number of 1-bits in an integer — called **popcount** (population count) or Hamming weight — is a fundamental operation. It appears in Hamming distance calculations (how many bits differ between two values), error-correcting codes, sparse bitset operations, and many competitive programming problems.

The naive approach checks every bit one by one: loop 32 times, test the lowest bit with `n & 1`, shift right. This always takes exactly 32 iterations regardless of how many bits are actually set.

**Brian Kernighan's improvement**: leverage the `n & (n-1)` trick to clear one set bit per iteration. The loop runs exactly k times where k is the number of set bits — which could be much less than 32 for sparse values.

```
function popcount(n) {
  let count = 0;
  while (n > 0) {
    n &= (n - 1); // clear the lowest set bit
    count++;
  }
  return count;
}
```

For a value like `0b10000001` (only 2 bits set), this runs in 2 iterations instead of 8. For a full byte `0xFF` (8 bits set), it runs 8 times — same as naive in the worst case. Optimised CPU instruction sets (x86 `POPCNT`, ARM `CNT`) make popcount a single-cycle hardware instruction in modern environments, but Kernighan's algorithm is the canonical software solution and is guaranteed to appear in interviews.

A third approach uses a **lookup table**: precompute popcount for all 256 possible byte values, then split a 32-bit integer into 4 bytes and look up each. This gives O(1) with constant overhead of 256 table entries. At the extreme, parallel bit tricks can compute popcount for a 32-bit integer in about 5 operations using the divide-and-conquer approach — summing adjacent 1-bit fields, then 2-bit fields, then 4-bit fields, etc.

